## Template for local.properties — copy to `local.properties` and fill in real values.
##
## local.properties is gitignored: it carries this machine's SDK path, the AppLovin
## credentials, and the release keystore password, none of which belong in the repo. This
## file is the committed record of WHICH keys a working checkout needs, so a fresh clone
## doesn't have to reverse-engineer them out of a Gradle failure.
##
##   cp local.properties.example local.properties
##
## Everything above the signing section is required. There is no local.defaults.properties,
## and app/build.gradle.kts declares no `secrets { }` block, so the secrets-gradle-plugin
## reads those straight out of local.properties — a missing one fails the build rather than
## falling back. The signing keys at the bottom are the exception: they are optional, and
## what happens without them is described there.

# Android SDK location. Written automatically by Android Studio on first open.
# Windows paths may use forward slashes; backslashes must be escaped (C\:\Users\you\...).
sdk.dir=/path/to/Android/Sdk

# AppLovin MAX — dashboard: https://dash.applovin.com/  (Account → Keys)
APPLOVIN_SDK_KEY=your-applovin-sdk-key

# AppLovin MAX ad unit IDs — dashboard: MAX → Manage → Ad Units
AD_UNIT_BANNER=your-banner-ad-unit-id
AD_UNIT_INTERSTITIAL=your-interstitial-ad-unit-id
AD_UNIT_NATIVE=your-native-ad-unit-id
AD_UNIT_APP_OPEN=your-app-open-ad-unit-id

# Google Play Billing product ID for the lifetime Pro unlock.
PRODUCT_LIFETIME=your-lifetime-product-id


## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Release signing — OPTIONAL, and read directly by app/build.gradle.kts rather than by the
## secrets plugin.
##
## Leave these out and `assembleRelease` / `bundleRelease` still work, producing an UNSIGNED
## artifact. That is deliberate: a clone without the keystore can still run a release build
## to check that R8 and resource shrinking pass. An unsigned AAB cannot be uploaded to Play.
##
## It is all four or none. Three out of four is treated as "not configured" and silently
## produces an unsigned build, because a partial config otherwise fails deep inside the
## packaging task with an error that never names the missing property.
##
## Each key also falls back to an environment variable of the same name, which is how CI
## signs without a local.properties file at all. local.properties wins where both are set.
##
## NEVER commit the filled-in values, and keep the .jks itself outside the repo.
## To recover the alias from an existing keystore (prompts for the password in your own
## terminal, and prints nothing secret):
##
##   keytool -list -v -keystore /path/to/your.jks
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Absolute path to the upload keystore. Not a secret in itself, but machine-specific.
RELEASE_STORE_FILE=/path/to/your/keystore.jks

# Keystore password, the key's alias, and that key's own password. On a keystore created by
# Android Studio the store and key passwords are often the same; they need not be.
RELEASE_STORE_PASSWORD=your-keystore-password
RELEASE_KEY_ALIAS=your-key-alias
RELEASE_KEY_PASSWORD=your-key-password
